# Leave every value in this file bare — no surrounding single or double
# quotes. python-dotenv treats quoting as meaningful, not cosmetic:
# unquoted (this file's convention throughout) strips surrounding
# whitespace with no escape processing; single-quoted is fully literal;
# double-quoted processes escape sequences like \n. None of this project's
# values (API keys, ISO dates, host:port pairs) need any of that. The one
# case quoting would matter is a value containing a literal '#' — unquoted,
# everything from that '#' onward is parsed as a comment, not part of the
# value.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Optional: free-tier providers tried before OPENAI_API_KEY above, in the
# order configured in config/model-selection.json's provider_chain. Any of
# these left unset is simply skipped (see GET /providers/status to confirm
# what's actually configured) — none are required for the app to work.
#
# One separate env var per provider, deliberately — never overload
# OPENAI_API_KEY for these. "OpenAI-compatible" describes a request/response
# protocol shape (most of these servers accept the OpenAI SDK's client
# shape at a different base_url), not the provider's identity or its own
# credential. Each URL below was checked directly against that provider's
# own current docs (not a secondhand summary) on 2026-09-14.
#
# Optional per-provider companion: "{VAR}_EXPIRES=YYYY-MM-DD" (e.g.
# GROQ_API_KEY_EXPIRES=2026-12-13), if that key has a known expiry — logged
# as a startup warning within providers.KEY_EXPIRY_WARNING_DAYS of it, and
# surfaced in GET /providers/status, instead of silently failing later as
# an ordinary auth error. Most of these providers' keys don't expire at
# all; only set this for one that does.

# Groq (GroqCloud) — recurring free tier, no card required. Confirmed
# (Groq's own docs) to support strict OpenAI-style structured output on
# the model this project uses — see provider_chain's "groq" entry.
# Get a key: https://console.groq.com/keys
GROQ_API_KEY=
# GROQ_API_KEY_EXPIRES=

# Gemini (Google AI Studio) — recurring free tier for eligible models.
# Get a key: https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Mistral (La Plateforme / Studio) — free "Experiment" mode, enabled by
# default, no credit card required (confirmed directly from Mistral's docs).
# Get a key: https://console.mistral.ai -> API Keys (left sidebar)
MISTRAL_API_KEY=

# OpenRouter — aggregates many free models behind one key; which specific
# model is actually free/available drifts over time (see provider_chain's
# "openrouter" entry notes).
# Get a key: https://openrouter.ai/keys
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# SambaNova (SambaCloud) — free tier only while no payment method is linked
# to the account; low daily request quota (see provider_chain's "sambanova"
# entry) — treat as a fallback/eval provider, not a primary one.
# Get a key: https://cloud.sambanova.ai/apis
SAMBANOVA_API_KEY=

# Cloudflare Workers AI needs BOTH of these, not just one.
# Get both: https://dash.cloudflare.com/?to=/:account/ai/workers-ai ->
# "Use REST API" (creates the token and shows your Account ID together)
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=

# Optional and opt-in: LAN-local OpenAI-compatible inference server(s) —
# e.g. a llama.cpp server on this machine or another one on the same
# network. If tried before every cloud provider above (see providers.py's
# economic utility frontier). Models are discovered live from each
# server's own /models endpoint; none are ever named in this repo's code
# or config.
#
# Leaving every LOCAL_INFERENCE_* var below unset/commented-out disables
# LAN-local entirely — it is never probed at a conventional default host
# just because this block is empty, since that would let a LAN server
# outside this app's knowledge silently win over OpenAI on every request.
#
# Single server: uncomment and set at least one of these two (whichever
# you leave out reuses the shown default for that one only):
# LOCAL_INFERENCE_HOST=127.0.0.1
# LOCAL_INFERENCE_PORT=8010
#
# More than one server: set LOCAL_INFERENCE_SERVERS instead, as a
# comma-separated host:port list — this takes priority over the two vars
# above when set.
# LOCAL_INFERENCE_SERVERS=127.0.0.1:8010,192.168.1.42:8011
#
# Most local servers (llama.cpp included) don't check this key at all —
# leave unset unless yours actually requires one.
# LOCAL_INFERENCE_API_KEY=

# Postgres, for Deliverable 12.8's retrieval_run persistence (see
# db.py). Same pattern as the VERA capstone project: INTERNAL_DB_URL only
# resolves from inside Render's private network (used automatically when
# RENDER=true, which Render sets itself — never set RENDER yourself).
# EXTERNAL_DB_URL is what local/off-Render runs use; it requires your
# current IP to be allowed in the database's Inbound IP Restrictions.
# This is a SEPARATE Render Postgres instance from VERA's — a deliberate
# project-boundary choice, not an oversight, so tearing down either
# project's database never affects the other.
# DATABASE SETTINGS: locally, put everything from here down to the admin
# block in .env.db-accounts, not .env. db.py loads that file itself
# (gitignored, mode 600); on Render, set the same names as service env vars.
# Preferred (p3m3 item #47): the URL is built from parts by db.build_url,
#   postgresql://DB_ACCOUNT:DB_PASSWORD@DB_HOST[DB_HOST_EXTERNAL_SUFFIX]/DB_NAME
# Render's internal hostname is the bare host (dpg-<id>-a), used when
# RENDER=true; off-Render the external one is host + regional suffix
# (e.g. .oregon-postgres.render.com). DB_ACCOUNT should be a least-privilege
# _rw account, never the admin. When DB_ACCOUNT is unset, db.py falls back to
# the full INTERNAL_DB_URL / EXTERNAL_DB_URL below.
DB_ACCOUNT=
DB_PASSWORD=
DB_HOST=
DB_HOST_EXTERNAL_SUFFIX=
DB_NAME=
INTERNAL_DB_URL=
EXTERNAL_DB_URL=

# Least-privilege accounts (p3m3 item #47, OWASP ASI03). DB_ACCOUNT above
# should be a read/write account with no admin rights: DB_API_RW_USER on
# the Render API service, DB_LOCAL_AGENT_RW_USER for local runs by the coding
# agent and its scripts. Name accounts after the agent, process or tool that
# uses them, not a person. scripts/provision_db_accounts.py creates them and
# upserts DB_ACCOUNT_PASSWORD_<account> lines into .env.db-accounts.
# No account name is hardcoded anywhere; all come from here.
DB_RO_GROUP=internship_ro
DB_RW_GROUP=internship_rw
DB_API_RW_USER=internship_api_rw
DB_LOCAL_AGENT_RW_USER=internship_claude_code_rw
# Backups (best practice; optional). Take a pg_dump of the internship schema
# before any production change and on a schedule, run as its own read-only
# account (pg_dump needs only SELECT) so a leaked backup credential can't
# change data. pg_dump's major version must be >= the server's (PostgreSQL 18
# here). Set DB_BACKUP_RO_USER and provision_db_accounts.py also creates the
# account and upserts its password line. Skipped for the course (ends 2026-10-12):
# the corpus is rebuildable from sources, but events/document_versions
# history is not, so a real deployment should back up.
# DB_BACKUP_RO_USER=internship_backup_ro
# DB_ACCOUNTS_OUT=.env.db-accounts  (override the settings file path)

# Admin account: schema changes and account management only (db.get_admin_engine;
# install_schema, create_vector_indexes, provision_db_accounts.py). Never used by
# the running app; keep it out of Render's service environment. Uses the same
# DB_HOST/DB_NAME; DB_ADMIN_ROLE is tried first, and DB_ADMIN_ROLE_RETRY (same
# password) if that login is rejected, e.g. after the hosting provider renames
# the admin account.
DB_ADMIN_ROLE=
DB_ADMIN_ROLE_RETRY=
DB_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# API_BASE_URL is read by the Streamlit UI (MVP_Layered_Ask.py and pages/),
# not main.py — and the UI never calls load_dotenv(), so setting it in THIS
# file does nothing locally. It only takes effect as a real OS/shell env var,
# e.g. exported before `streamlit run MVP_Layered_Ask.py`, or as a dashboard
# env var on a deployed Streamlit service (see README's Deploy section).
# Left unset, the sidebar falls back to .faststream-local-url, then
# http://127.0.0.1:8000.
# API_BASE_URL=

# --- /ingest authentication (optional) ---
# Callers sending a matching X-Ingest-Key header count as authenticated:
# content flagged as possible prompt injection is then accepted, and their
# re-ingests of an existing document_id are auto-accepted when the content
# scan is clean. Unauthenticated callers get flagged content rejected and
# re-ingests staged for review. If this is UNSET, every caller counts as
# authenticated. Compared in constant time.
# INGEST_API_KEY=

# --- /ask retrieval switches (optional; defaults are the tested ones) ---
# Read at request time, so changing one on the host and restarting is
# enough, no redeploy. Evidence for the defaults: README, "Retrieval
# decisions".
# Reranker on/off (default on).
# RAG_RERANK=1
# Reranker model, pinned to the lowest-cost model (default gpt-4.1-nano).
# RAG_RERANK_MODEL=gpt-4.1-nano
# Skip the reranker when the top document leads the next document's best
# chunk by more than this distance (default 0.20, calibrated on the
# 200-question eval).
# RAG_RERANK_HIGH_GAP=0.20
# Hybrid BM25 + dense retrieval (default off). Adds ~232 MB of memory for
# the in-process BM25 index (~600 MB peak during a post-ingest rebuild),
# which exceeds a 512 MB free-tier instance.
# RAG_HYBRID=0

# --- /agent audit log content (optional) ---
# Every /agent run writes one agent_run event with metadata only (tool
# name, outcome, error type, document IDs, duration). Set this to also log
# the question and the tool's arguments and results. Off by default,
# following OpenTelemetry's GenAI guidance: that content can hold
# sensitive text.
# AGENT_LOG_TOOL_CONTENT=0
